# Normalize line endings.
#
# Without this, files authored on Linux (LF) and committed from Windows with
# core.autocrlf=true produce whole-file phantom diffs: `git diff` reported
# 1080 changed lines across seven files during Phase 1 when the only real
# change was two lines in main.py. A review that has to find two real lines
# inside a thousand fake ones is a review that stops happening.
#
# LF in the repository for everything text; Git still checks out CRLF on
# Windows where a tool needs it. This is the standard fix, and it matters
# more here than in a single-machine project because this repo is edited
# from both Linux and Windows.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts must keep CRLF on disk — PowerShell and batch files
# can misbehave with LF endings depending on host and encoding. V2 Module 6
# adds scripts/powershell/, so this is set before those land rather than
# after something breaks.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.psm1 text eol=crlf
*.psd1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts and Dockerfiles must keep LF — a CRLF shebang line makes a
# container fail at startup with an unhelpful "no such file or directory".
*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.dockerfile text eol=lf

# Binary — never diff, never normalize.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
